        "2586861e5b10403db7aea7c70705461c",
        "eebbb681691d4c0dbc12635a1a6a4152"
      ],
      "metrics": {},
      "notes": "targeted challenger: only preprocess rewritten (parent v2). Rewrote the prompt from a rule-and-example list into an explicit five-step decomposition procedure (name quantities \u2192 resolve references against history \u2192 classify operands as cached/lookup/literal \u2192 pick the operation from the question's verb \u2192 order operands and self-check), adding coverage for verdict questions via `greater`, level-vs-change ratios, combined \"X and Y\" line items, enumerated-component totals, conventional constants, scale-conversion direction, impossible/typo periods, already-percent referents, and operand ordering; the three output fields, letter mapping, numeric-only program, and the existing ratio/percentage conventions are retained unchanged.",
      "parent": "v2",
      "target_agent": "preprocess",
      "rationale": "The current prompt is organized as a list of output-format rules plus a gallery of percentage patterns; it teaches the agent how to *format* a program but never how to *decide* one, so every failure class here is a decision the prompt simply does not cover \u2014 which referent \"that value\" points at, whether an operand is a lookup or a document convention constant, whether a relation question wants a difference or a bare quotient, which operand becomes the minuend, and whether the turn is a comparison at all. Because the prompt also declares that only add/subtract/multiply/divide exist, the agent explicitly reasoned itself into emitting a subtraction on a verdict question; the calculator already exposes a `greater` tool, so admitting `greater` into the program vocabulary costs nothing downstream and removes an impossible constraint. I therefore restructured the prompt around a five-step decision procedure \u2014 name the quantities, resolve references against history, classify each operand as cached/lookup/literal, choose the operation from the question's verb, then order operands and self-check \u2014 and folded the original percentage/ratio conventions in as the final wrapper step so the output contract, the four arithmetic functions, the letter-to-sub-question mapping, the numeric-only program, and all three output fields are preserved verbatim in requirement. The generic worked shapes replace the old year-specific examples so the guidance generalizes rather than pattern-matching a handful of remembered questions.",
      "changed_agents": [
        "preprocess"
      ],
      "composition": "t2.p5.r2.c2",
      "campaign": "c01"
    },
