      "metrics": {},
      "notes": "targeted challenger: only preprocess rewritten (parent v2). Restructured the prompt around a five-step decomposition procedure (single-cell level lookups \u2192 reference resolution with a vacuity check \u2192 operand classification as cached level/delta/lookup/literal \u2192 operation choice from the question's verb \u2192 operand ordering and read-back), while preserving the three output fields, letter mapping, the four-function numeric-only DSL, all five original Program Design Rules, and the original worked examples verbatim. Compared with the rejected v6 it omits the `greater` operator and the speculative conjoined-metric, adjustment-qualifier, corrupted-period and percent-form rules, and restores the concrete examples v6 deleted.",
      "parent": "v2",
      "target_agent": "preprocess",
      "rationale": "The current prompt is a formatting contract plus a gallery of percentage patterns: it tells the agent how to *write* a program but never how to *decide* one, so the recurring faults \u2014 re-differencing an operand that is already a delta, dividing a rate-valued metric by its own base instead of taking percentage points, phrasing a sub-question as \"change in X\" when the program needs X's level, phrasing one sub-question as an aggregate over two periods, collapsing a genuinely computational turn to a one-operand `A`, binding \"this change\" to the wrong turn, and inverting numerator/denominator \u2014 are all decisions the prompt is silent on. The previous restructure (v6) moved in the right direction (+2.29pp, P(\u0394>0)=0.79) but was rejected at p=0.19 with 23 questions broken against 31 fixed, so the fix is not \"more procedure\" but *less collateral damage*: this rewrite keeps the procedural spine that trended positive while deliberately dropping v6's speculative and contract-touching additions \u2014 the `greater` operator (an output-contract change), the \"metrics joined by and\" heuristic, adjustment-qualifier decomposition, corrupted-period repair, and the percent-form re-derivation rule \u2014 and it restores v6's deleted concrete examples and the original percentage/ratio conventions verbatim, since those encode baseline-calibrated dataset behaviour. Every rule I do add is backed by at least one, mostly two, independently diagnosed failures in this batch, and I deliberately left the \u00d7100-vs-bare-quotient policy untouched because the evidence points in both directions on identical phrasings, making any change there a coin flip that would reintroduce exactly the kind of breakage that sank v6.",
      "changed_agents": [
        "preprocess"
      ],
      "composition": "t2.p6.r2.c2",
      "campaign": "c01"
    }
